--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
static assertion failed
--
N5008 [expr.spaceship]/2, /6: for pointer operands `p <=> q' is a PRVALUE of
type std::strong_ordering, which is std::strong_ordering::equal if p and q
compare equal ([expr.eq]), otherwise less or greater according to [expr.rel]
(unspecified for pointers to different complete objects, /6.3 -- but never
equal).  The front end typed the result like the `static constexpr' member
it yields (const std::strong_ordering), so libc++'s
`__compares_as<decltype((a <=> b)), partial_ordering>' saw a const type,
classified it as no comparison category and rejected
std::compare_three_way()(p, q); and it tested `<' and `>' first, so two
pointers to different objects (unordered in cbmc's model) came out equal.
KNOWNBUG cpp20_compare_three_way_pointers_libcxx.  Fixed in round 163b.
